Diagnosis: `com.google.javascript.jscomp.CheckSideEffects.visit(com.google.javascript.jscomp.NodeTraversal, com.google.javascript.rhino.Node, com.google.javascript.rhino.Node)` is the buggy method.

The failure is in its special handling for nodes whose `parent` is `Token.COMMA` at [CheckSideEffects.java:113] and especially [CheckSideEffects.java:117]. For the failing input `a = (bar(), 6, 7)`, the AST is effectively nested as a comma inside another comma. The literal `6` is the last child of the inner comma, so this block runs:

```java
if (n == parent.getLastChild()) {
  for (Node an : parent.getAncestors()) {
    int ancestorType = an.getType();
    if (ancestorType == Token.COMMA) continue;
    if (ancestorType != Token.EXPR_RESULT && ancestorType != Token.BLOCK) return;
    else break;
  }
}
```

That logic is wrong for nested comma expressions. It sees that the inner comma’s result flows into an ancestor that is not `EXPR_RESULT`/`BLOCK` and returns early, so no warning is emitted for `6`. But semantically the value of that inner comma is still discarded by the outer comma, so `6` is useless and should be reported.

Why this is the root cause:
- `NodeUtil.isExpressionResultUsed(n)` already has comma-specific recursion and would classify `6` as unused in this shape.
- `visit(...)` overrides that correct result with extra comma logic and returns before reaching the warning/reporting block at [CheckSideEffects.java:130].
- The symptom matches exactly: no warning for the middle operand of a multi-term comma expression.

Best fix location:

`CheckSideEffects.visit(com.google.javascript.jscomp.NodeTraversal, com.google.javascript.rhino.Node, com.google.javascript.rhino.Node)`
